/* data/learn-c/phy-12-01.js - GENERATED by tools/build_learn.py from data/learn/summaries/phy-12-01.json (our own words;
 * the notes of one chapter, loaded when the chapter is opened). Do not edit by hand. */
(window.LEARN_C = window.LEARN_C || {})["phy-12-01"] = {"1.1":{"s":"This chapter is about things that go round. Revolution means moving in a circle around a point outside the body, like the Moon around the Earth. Rotation means turning about an axis through the body itself, like a spinning fan."},"1.2":{"s":"Circular motion is always accelerated, because the direction of velocity keeps changing. It is also periodic. We describe it with angular displacement, angular velocity (ω) and angular acceleration, the rotational partners of s, v and a. Linear speed and angular speed are linked by v = ωr. In uniform circular motion the speed is constant and the acceleration points to the centre: a = v²/r = ω²r.","f":[{"x":"v = omega*r","n":"Linear speed from angular speed; r = radius of the circle.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 1.2, p.2"},{"x":"F = m*v^2/r = m*r*omega^2","n":"Size of the centripetal force for uniform circular motion.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 1.2, p.3"}],"w":["Centripetal force is not a new kind of force; it is the resultant of the real forces, pointing to the centre.","Centrifugal force appears only in the rotating frame; never add both in one equation."]},"1.3":{"s":"Uses of uniform circular motion. On a flat curved road, friction alone gives the centripetal force, so there is a safe speed limit. On a banked road the tilt of the road helps; the best speed with no friction needs tan θ = v²/(rg). A two-wheeler rider leans inward for the same reason. A conical pendulum and the 'well of death' are other examples.","f":[{"x":"v_max = sqrt(mu_s*r*g)","n":"Flat curved road: highest speed before skidding; mu_s = coefficient of static friction.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 1.3, p.4"},{"x":"v_min = sqrt(r*g/mu_s)","n":"Well of death: lowest speed that stops the rider sliding down.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 1.3, p.5"},{"x":"tan(theta) = v^2/(r*g)","n":"Banked road, no friction needed: the most safe speed v_s = sqrt(r*g*tan(theta)).","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 1.3, p.6"},{"x":"v_max = sqrt(r*g*(tan(theta) + mu_s)/(1 - mu_s*tan(theta)))","n":"Banked road with friction, upper limit; valid while mu_s*tan(theta) < 1. Lower limit: swap the signs; v_min = 0 if mu_s >= tan(theta).","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 1.3, p.7"},{"x":"T = 2*pi*sqrt(L*cos(theta)/g)","n":"Conical pendulum: period; L = string length, theta = angle of the string with the vertical.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 1.3, p.8"}],"w":["Mass cancels: the safe speed of a curve does not depend on the vehicle's mass.","The string of a conical pendulum can never be exactly horizontal."]},"1.4":{"s":"Vertical circular motion, like a bob whirled on a string. Speed is not constant: gravity changes kinetic energy into potential energy and back. The string is slackest at the top. To just complete the circle, the speed at the top must be at least √(rg), which means at least √(5rg) at the bottom.","f":[{"x":"v_top = sqrt(r*g)","n":"Mass on a string: least speed at the top to complete the circle (tension just zero).","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 1.4, p.10"},{"x":"v_bottom = sqrt(5*r*g)","n":"Matching least speed at the lowest point.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 1.4, p.10"},{"x":"T_bottom - T_top = 6*m*g","n":"Difference in string tension between the lowest and highest points.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 1.4, p.10"},{"x":"v_max = sqrt(g*r)","n":"Convex over-bridge: above this speed the vehicle loses contact at the top (N = 0).","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 1.4, p.11"}],"w":["A rod (not a string) can push, so the least speed at the top is about zero and 2√(rg) at the bottom.","Speed changes around a vertical circle, so use energy conservation, not v = constant."]},"1.5":{"s":"In rotation, moment of inertia (I) plays the part that mass plays in straight-line motion. It tells how hard it is to change an object's rotation. It depends on the mass and on how far that mass is from the axis. For particles, I = Σ m r². Rotational kinetic energy is ½ I ω².","f":[{"x":"I = Sigma m_i*r_i^2","n":"Moment of inertia of a set of particles; r_i = perpendicular distance from the axis.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 1.5, p.13"},{"x":"KE = (1/2)*I*omega^2","n":"Rotational kinetic energy.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 1.5, p.13"},{"x":"I = M*R^2","n":"Thin ring about its central axis.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 1.5, p.13"},{"x":"I = (1/2)*M*R^2","n":"Uniform disc about its central axis.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 1.5, p.14"}],"w":["Moment of inertia always belongs to a stated axis; the same body has a different I about a different axis."]},"1.6":{"s":"Radius of gyration (K) is the distance from the axis at which the whole mass could be placed to give the same moment of inertia. So I = M K². A larger K means the mass is spread farther from the axis.","f":[{"x":"I = M*K^2","n":"K = radius of gyration.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 1.6, p.14"},{"x":"K = R/sqrt(2)","n":"Disc about its central axis; for a ring K = R.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 1.6, p.14"}],"w":["For the same M and R, a ring has a larger K (and I) than a disc: its mass is all at the rim."]},"1.7":{"s":"Two shortcuts for moment of inertia about a new axis. Parallel axes: I = I(cm) + M h², where h is the distance from the axis through the centre of mass. Perpendicular axes (flat objects only): I(z) = I(x) + I(y). Here x and y lie in the plane, z is perpendicular to it, and all three meet at one point.","f":[{"x":"I_O = I_C + M*h^2","n":"Parallel axes: I_C about the parallel axis through the centre of mass; h = distance between the axes.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 1.7, p.15"},{"x":"I_z = I_x + I_y","n":"Perpendicular axes: flat (laminar) bodies only; x, y in the plane, z perpendicular, all meeting at one point.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 1.7, p.16"}],"w":["Parallel axes needs one axis through the centre of mass; it cannot jump between two other axes directly.","Perpendicular axes does not work for a solid sphere or cylinder: only for flat bodies."]},"1.8":{"s":"Angular momentum is the moment of linear momentum about an axis: L = r × p. For a rigid body turning about a fixed axis, L = I ω. It is the rotational partner of linear momentum p = m v.","f":[{"x":"L = r*p*sin(theta)","n":"Angular momentum of a particle; theta = angle between r and p.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 1.8, p.17"},{"x":"L = I*omega","n":"Rigid body about a fixed axis.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 1.8, p.17"}]},"1.9":{"s":"Torque is the rotational partner of force. For a rigid body about a fixed axis, torque = I α, where α is the angular acceleration, just as F = m a. Torque also equals the rate of change of angular momentum.","f":[{"x":"tau = I*alpha","n":"Torque about a fixed axis; alpha = angular acceleration (like F = m*a).","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 1.9, p.17"}]},"1.10":{"s":"If no external torque acts, angular momentum stays constant: I₁ω₁ = I₂ω₂. A skater pulling in her arms makes I smaller, so she spins faster. A diver tucks in to turn faster in the air.","f":[{"x":"tau = dL/dt","n":"Torque is the rate of change of angular momentum.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 1.10, p.18"},{"x":"I_1*omega_1 = I_2*omega_2","n":"No external torque: angular momentum stays the same.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 1.10, p.19"}],"w":["When I falls, ω rises, but the rotational KE = L²/(2I) rises too: the body does work pulling inward."]},"1.11":{"s":"Rolling is rotation and straight-line motion together. Total kinetic energy = ½ m v² + ½ I ω², which can be written ½ m v² (1 + K²/R²). Let objects roll down a slope from rest. The one with the smaller K²/R² is faster at the bottom and reaches first, so a solid sphere beats a ring.","f":[{"x":"E = (1/2)*M*v^2*(1 + K^2/R^2)","n":"Total kinetic energy of a rolling body (translation + rotation).","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 1.11, p.19"},{"x":"v = sqrt(2*g*h/(1 + K^2/R^2))","n":"Speed after rolling down a height h from rest without slipping.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 1.11, p.20"},{"x":"a = g*sin(theta)/(1 + K^2/R^2)","n":"Acceleration down an incline of angle theta when rolling.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 1.11, p.20"},{"x":"K^2/R^2 = 1, 1/2, 2/5, 2/3","n":"Ring or hollow cylinder, disc or solid cylinder, solid sphere, hollow sphere.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 1.11, p.20"}],"w":["Smaller K²/R² reaches the bottom first: solid sphere, then disc, then hollow sphere, then ring.","Pure rolling needs static friction, but it does no work, so energy is conserved."]}};
